WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:01.824
<v Examiner>Smell.

2
00:00:01.824 --> 00:00:02.583
<v Examiner>Five marks.

3
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:06.708
<v Examiner>Briefly describe the structure of the olfactory epithelium,

4
00:00:06.708 --> 00:00:09.199
<v Examiner>and discuss the physiology of olfaction.

5
00:00:09.649 --> 00:00:13.041
Taste's twin, and also her learning outcome, word for word.

6
00:00:13.491 --> 00:00:17.843
The hook: roof, mucus, receptor, cribriform plate, bulb, temporal lobe.

7
00:00:18.293 --> 00:00:19.188
The structure.

8
00:00:19.188 --> 00:00:23.152
The olfactory epithelium lies in the roof of the nasal cavity.

9
00:00:23.152 --> 00:00:28.906
It sits so high that most of the air you breathe never reaches it, which is why you sniff.

10
00:00:28.906 --> 00:00:34.469
It holds the olfactory receptor neurons, which are chemoreceptors with olfactory cilia.

11
00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:39.903
Plus supporting cells, and basal cells, which regularly replace the receptor neurons.

12
00:00:40.353 --> 00:00:40.991
Olfaction.

13
00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:46.535
Odourants are breathed in, and dissolve in the mucus covering the olfactory epithelium.

14
00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:51.952
Then they bind to receptors on the olfactory receptor neurons, which starts a signal.

15
00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:56.859
The neurons' axons pass up through the cribriform plate as cranial nerve one,

16
00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:00.364
the olfactory nerve, and synapse in the olfactory bulb.

17
00:01:01.814 --> 00:01:04.781
The olfactory bulbs lie under the frontal lobe,

18
00:01:04.781 --> 00:01:07.684
and the olfactory tract carries the signal on.

19
00:01:07.684 --> 00:01:12.292
The primary olfactory cortex, in the temporal lobe, identifies the smell.

20
00:01:12.292 --> 00:01:16.964
The amygdala ties smell to emotion, and the hippocampus ties it to memory.

21
00:01:16.964 --> 00:01:20.246
That's why a smell can bring a memory straight back.

22
00:01:20.696 --> 00:01:23.081
Complete loss of smell is anosmia.

23
00:01:23.081 --> 00:01:24.835
Partial loss is hyposmia.

24
00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:26.958
The traps.

25
00:01:26.958 --> 00:01:30.189
Smell is cranial nerve one, the olfactory nerve.

26
00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:31.738
Two is the optic nerve.

27
00:01:31.738 --> 00:01:36.181
One of her true or false statements says the second cranial nerve.

28
00:01:36.181 --> 00:01:36.585
False.

29
00:01:36.585 --> 00:01:41.364
Second: the receptors are the olfactory receptor cells and their cilia,

30
00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:43.653
not the basal or supporting cells.

31
00:01:43.653 --> 00:01:46.615
Third: it's in the roof of the nasal cavity.

32
00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:47.772
<v Examiner>Once more.

33
00:01:47.772 --> 00:01:48.196
<v Examiner>Smell.

34
00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:56.206
The olfactory epithelium is in the roof of the nasal cavity, which is why you sniff.

35
00:01:56.206 --> 00:02:00.376
It has olfactory receptor neurons with cilia, supporting cells,

36
00:02:00.376 --> 00:02:03.090
and basal cells that replace the neurons.

37
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.731
Odourants dissolve in the mucus and bind the receptors.

38
00:02:06.731 --> 00:02:11.033
The axons pass through the cribriform plate as cranial nerve one,

39
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:14.873
and synapse in the olfactory bulb, under the frontal lobe.

40
00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:19.572
The primary olfactory cortex in the temporal lobe identifies the smell;

41
00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:23.147
the amygdala adds emotion, and the hippocampus memory.

42
00:02:23.147 --> 00:02:26.324
Total loss is anosmia; partial loss is hyposmia.
